{% llm_copy_prompt title="Migrate Nx Module Federation to the official plugins" %}
Migrate this workspace off the Nx Module Federation APIs that Nx v24 will remove, keeping each app's current bundler. Follow every step at {pageUrl} in order. Scope is React client-side webpack and Rspack. Stop and report if you find Angular or server-side rendering apps.

Rules that override any shortcut you are tempted to take:

1. Before editing, run production builds and keep every `dist/apps/<app>/mf-stats.json`. Its `shared` array, `exposes`, and container name are what you reproduce. Do not derive sharing from package.json.
2. Replace `withModuleFederation` / `NxModuleFederationPlugin` with `ModuleFederationPlugin` from `@module-federation/enhanced/webpack` or `/rspack`. Keep `NxAppWebpackPlugin`, `NxAppRspackPlugin`, `NxReactWebpackPlugin`, `NxReactRspackPlugin`. Do not switch bundlers or regenerate apps.
3. Write `remotes` as explicit `alias: 'container@url/remoteEntry.js'` entries. The container name is the project name with every character outside `[a-zA-Z0-9_$]` and any leading digit replaced by `_`; the alias is the original project name. Fold `webpack.config.prod.*` tuple URLs into the same config keyed on `NODE_ENV`, appending `remoteEntry.js` to bare origins with exactly one slash before it. Drop any `library` entry.
4. Pass `dts: false` and `filename: 'remoteEntry.js'`, and on webpack `remoteType: 'script'`.
5. For runtime-loaded remotes, convert `setRemoteDefinitions` to `registerRemotes` with `type: 'global'` and `loadRemoteModule(name, './Module')` to `loadRemote(name + '/Module')`. Every map value must be a full `remoteEntry.js` URL. Hosts that already call `registerRemotes` stay as they are.
6. Rewrite relative imports that reach into another workspace library to that library's public entry point. Nx rewrote those for you and the plugin does not.
7. In app-plugin options set `runtimeChunk: false` and `commonChunk: false`. Set `devServer.hot: true` on every app. Set `output.uniqueName`, `output.publicPath: 'auto'`, `output.clean: true`, and on webpack `output.scriptType: 'text/javascript'`. On Rspack set `lazyCompilation: false`.
8. Move `build` and `serve` to `nx:run-commands` running the bundler CLI, with `NODE_ENV` per configuration. Add `webpack-cli` if missing. Add a `serve-static` target per app using `@nx/web:file-server` with `staticFilePath`, `dependsOn: ['build']`, `watch: false`, never `buildTarget`. Point any e2e `webServerCommand` at `nx run-many -t serve-static` across host and remotes.
9. Hosts that load remotes from a runtime map get `implicitDependencies` on those remotes.
10. Remove `@nx/module-federation` and the `@nx/react/mf`, `@nx/react/module-federation`, `@nx/rspack/module-federation` imports only after every consumer is gone. Move `@module-federation/enhanced` to `dependencies`. Rebuild uncached with the old package renamed away.
11. Do not claim hot-update parity, shared-state parity, or deployment-path correctness from a green build. Report what you verified and what you did not.

Reference: {pageUrl}
{% /llm_copy_prompt %}

Nx v24 will remove the Module Federation wrappers, runtime helpers, and dev-server executors that
`@nx/module-federation`, `@nx/react`, `@nx/rspack`, and `@nx/angular` ship today, and the
generators and executors that created these setups are already deprecated in v23.
Their replacement is the official [Module Federation](https://module-federation.io) plugins, which
`withModuleFederation` already wraps internally, so your webpack apps stay on webpack and your
Rspack apps stay on Rspack.

Run this migration before you upgrade to Nx v24, while the old packages still work and you can
compare behavior against a baseline.

The other way off these APIs is to regenerate each app with the `@nx/react:consumer` and
`@nx/react:provider` generators, covered in
[consumer and provider](/docs/kb/consumer-and-provider). Those generators emit Vite, Rsbuild, or
Rspack, so a webpack app changes bundler. Take the steps below when you want to keep the bundler
and the app you have.

{% aside type="note" title="Scope" %}
React client-side rendering on webpack or Rspack, keeping the same bundler.
Angular Module Federation and server-side rendering are not covered here.
For Angular, see [migrate Angular Module Federation](/docs/kb/migrate-angular-module-federation).
{% /aside %}

## What replaces what

| Going away in v24                                                                     | Replacement                                                         |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `withModuleFederation` from `@nx/module-federation/webpack`                           | `ModuleFederationPlugin` from `@module-federation/enhanced/webpack` |
| `withModuleFederation` from `@nx/module-federation/rspack`                            | `ModuleFederationPlugin` from `@module-federation/enhanced/rspack`  |
| `NxModuleFederationPlugin` from `@nx/module-federation/rspack`                        | `ModuleFederationPlugin` from `@module-federation/enhanced/rspack`  |
| `setRemoteDefinitions`, `setRemoteDefinition` from `@nx/react/mf`                     | `registerRemotes` from `@module-federation/enhanced/runtime`        |
| `loadRemoteModule` from `@nx/react/mf`                                                | `loadRemote` from `@module-federation/enhanced/runtime`             |
| `@nx/react:module-federation-dev-server` and the Rspack equivalent                    | `nx:run-commands` running your bundler dev server                   |
| `@nx/react:module-federation-static-server` and the Rspack equivalent                 | None yet. See [what you lose](#what-you-lose).                      |
| `@nx/react/module-federation` and `@nx/rspack/module-federation` re-exports           | Same as the `@nx/module-federation` entry they re-export            |
| `sharePackages`, `shareWorkspaceLibraries`, `mapRemotes` from `@nx/module-federation` | None. Write the resolved values out, Steps 3 and 5.                 |
| `NxModuleFederationDevServerPlugin`                                                   | None yet. See [what you lose](#what-you-lose).                      |

Keep `NxAppWebpackPlugin`, `NxAppRspackPlugin`, `NxReactWebpackPlugin`, and
`NxReactRspackPlugin`, which are general bundler plugins and are not part of this removal.

## Capture a baseline first

A remote can render correctly while the host and the remote hold separate copies of a shared
library, and a dev server can reload the whole page on every remote edit instead of hot-updating
it.
Both of those failures build green and show nothing in the output, so record the current behavior
before you change anything:

- Production builds for every host and remote, with the output file list per app, and each
  app's `serve` port, which the development remote URLs in Step 3 use.
- Each host rendered in a browser with every remote visible and its React hooks working.
- A shared mutable value changed in the host and read back in each remote.
- The requests for `remoteEntry.js` and remote chunks, including any deployment path prefix.
- Which remotes start when you serve the host, and whether editing a remote keeps host state.
- `dist/apps/<app>/mf-stats.json` from a production build into an empty `dist`, copied out of
  `dist` before you rebuild. It holds the resolved `shared` map, `exposes`, and normalized
  container name that Steps 3 and 5 reproduce.

If you are already on v24 and the project graph no longer loads, read the configuration files
directly and recover the old values from version control. A broken graph does not block the repair.

## Step 1: inventory the workspace

Search for every consumer before you edit one:

```shell
grep -rE "@nx/module-federation|@nx/react/module-federation|@nx/rspack/module-federation|@nx/react/mf|module-federation-dev-server|module-federation-static-server|module-federation-ssr-dev-server|NX_MF_DEV_REMOTES" --include="*.ts" --include="*.tsx" --include="*.js" --include="*.json" --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=.nx --exclude-dir=dist --exclude=package-lock.json --exclude=pnpm-lock.yaml --exclude=yarn.lock .
```

Record, per app, whether it exposes modules, consumes them, or does both, along with its
production remote URLs, custom upstream options, and any runtime plugins.
Flag Angular and server-side rendering apps now.

## Step 2: install the official packages

The `host` generator already added `@module-federation/enhanced` to your root `package.json`.
Move it under `dependencies` by hand if it sits in `devDependencies`, since the federation
runtime is imported by application code, then run your package manager's install so the lockfile
follows. Only if it is missing:

```shell
npm add --save-prod @module-federation/enhanced
```

Step 7 runs the bundler CLI directly. A webpack workspace that only ever built through the Nx
executor has no `webpack-cli`, even though `node_modules/.bin/webpack` exists, so add it now:

```shell
npm add -D webpack-cli
```

## Step 3: translate the federation config

Nx accepts a list of project names and resolves each one to a URL from the project graph.
The official plugin takes explicit entries, so resolve the names and ports yourself.

Two values that Nx derived are easy to lose here:

- The container name is normalized.
  Every character outside `[a-zA-Z0-9_$]` becomes `_`, and so does a leading digit, so a
  project named `webpack-host` publishes as `webpack_host`.
  Use the normalized form for `name`.
- The remote import alias is the original project name.
  `webpackRemoteA/Module` keeps working because the key in `remotes` is the alias, not the
  container name. Drop any `library` entry from the Nx config. Nx never passed it to the plugin,
  and the `{ type: 'var', name: '<project-name>' }` the generator wrote publishes an
  un-normalized global. On Rspack a `var` or `window` type was the switch for
  `remoteType: 'script'` and `output.scriptType: 'text/javascript'`, so carry those two by hand.

Nx also defaulted `filename` to `remoteEntry.js` and, for webpack, `remoteType` to `script`,
and it passed through the `{ dts: false }` override from your old `withModuleFederation` call.
Set all three explicitly. Without `dts: false`, every remote build logs
`[ Module Federation DTS ] Error ... #TYPE-001`.

A host that resolves its remotes at runtime has `remotes: []` in its Nx config. Give it
`remotes: {}` here and skip to Step 4 for the runtime side.

Generated webpack hosts also carry a `webpack.config.prod.*` file, wired through
`build.configurations.production.webpackConfig`, whose `remotes` are tuples such as
`['webpackRemoteA', 'http://localhost:4701/']`. Nx appended `remoteEntry.js` to any tuple URL
without a filename, with or without a trailing slash on the origin. Fold those URLs into the
single config, keyed on `NODE_ENV`, and write the full entry URL with exactly one slash before
`remoteEntry.js`:

```js
// apps/webpack-host/module-federation.config.js
const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production';
module.exports = {
  // ... name, filename, remoteType, dts
  remotes: {
    webpackRemoteA: isProd
      ? 'webpackRemoteA@https://remote-a.example.com/remoteEntry.js'
      : 'webpackRemoteA@http://localhost:4701/remoteEntry.js',
  },
};
```

Each app lists only its direct remotes, as before. Nx followed each remote's own federation
config to find remotes of remotes for serving only, so that set matters in Step 7, not here.
Treat a remote that also consumes remotes as both a provider and a consumer in every step below.

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Before" %}

```ts
// apps/webpack-host/module-federation.config.ts
import type { ModuleFederationConfig } from '@nx/module-federation';

const config: ModuleFederationConfig = {
  name: 'webpack-host',
  remotes: ['webpackRemoteA', 'webpackRemoteB'],
};

export default config;
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="After" %}

```js
// apps/webpack-host/module-federation.config.js
module.exports = {
  name: 'webpack_host',
  filename: 'remoteEntry.js',
  remoteType: 'script', // webpack only; on Rspack omit it, the plugin defaults to script
  dts: false,
  remotes: {
    webpackRemoteA: 'webpackRemoteA@http://localhost:4701/remoteEntry.js',
    webpackRemoteB: 'webpackRemoteB@http://localhost:4702/remoteEntry.js',
  },
  shared: {
    // ... see Step 5
  },
};
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

Remote configs keep their `exposes` map unchanged. Keep the `<remote>/Module` entries in
`tsconfig.base.json` paths and any `remotes.d.ts`: the type-check and the static
`import('<remote>/Module')` still rely on them. Delete the old `module-federation.config.ts`,
`webpack.config.ts`, and `webpack.config.prod.ts` (or the Rspack equivalents) once the new files
are in place, or the graph keeps reading them.

Then swap the wrapper for the plugin in the bundler config. The result is a plain config the
bundler CLI accepts, which the `composePlugins` form was not, so both the `build` and the
`serve` targets move to the CLI in Step 7. It still runs only under `nx run`: the app plugins
read `NX_TASK_TARGET_PROJECT` and friends to find the project, so `npx webpack serve` from the
app folder crashes.

The options on your old `build` target become `NxAppWebpackPlugin` or `NxAppRspackPlugin`
options. The plugin also merges whatever options the running target still carries in
`project.json`, and those win, so leave none of the old build options there. Paths such as
`main`, `index`, `tsConfig`, `assets`, and `styles` resolve from the project root, so
`apps/webpack-host/src/main.ts` becomes `./src/main.ts`, while `fileReplacements` paths resolve
from the workspace root; drop a `fileReplacements` pair whose files do not exist. Values that
differed per named configuration become `process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'` ternaries, which
Step 7 sets per configuration. The generator's per-configuration `optimization`,
`outputHashing`, `sourceMap`, `namedChunks`, and `vendorChunk` values match what the plugin
derives from `NODE_ENV` and can go, with the development-build caveat in Step 7;
`extractLicenses` has no such default, so keep its ternary.

{% tabs syncKey="mf-bundler" %}
{% tabitem label="webpack" %}

```js
// apps/webpack-host/webpack.config.js
const { NxAppWebpackPlugin } = require('@nx/webpack/app-plugin');
const { NxReactWebpackPlugin } = require('@nx/react/webpack-plugin');
const {
  ModuleFederationPlugin,
} = require('@module-federation/enhanced/webpack');
const { join } = require('node:path');
const mf = require('./module-federation.config');

module.exports = {
  output: {
    path: join(__dirname, '../../dist/apps/webpack-host'),
    publicPath: 'auto',
    uniqueName: 'webpack-host',
    scriptType: 'text/javascript',
    clean: true,
  },
  resolve: { modules: ['node_modules', join(__dirname, '../..')] },
  optimization: { runtimeChunk: false },
  devServer: {
    port: 4700,
    hot: true,
    static: false,
    headers: { 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*' },
    historyApiFallback: { index: '/index.html', disableDotRule: true },
  },
  plugins: [
    new NxAppWebpackPlugin({
      // ... your existing options, minus outputPath (output.path above replaces it)
      runtimeChunk: false,
      commonChunk: false,
    }),
    new NxReactWebpackPlugin(),
    new ModuleFederationPlugin(mf),
  ],
};
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Rspack" %}

```js
// apps/rspack-host/rspack.config.js
const { NxAppRspackPlugin } = require('@nx/rspack/app-plugin');
const { NxReactRspackPlugin } = require('@nx/rspack/react-plugin');
const {
  ModuleFederationPlugin,
} = require('@module-federation/enhanced/rspack');
const { join } = require('node:path');
const mf = require('./module-federation.config');

module.exports = {
  // rspack-cli turns this on in dev mode and it breaks Module Federation
  lazyCompilation: false,
  output: {
    path: join(__dirname, '../../dist/apps/rspack-host'),
    publicPath: 'auto',
    uniqueName: 'rspack-host',
    clean: true,
  },
  resolve: { modules: ['node_modules', join(__dirname, '../..')] },
  optimization: {
    runtimeChunk: false,
    // only if the old config used NxModuleFederationPlugin, which set these; withModuleFederation did not
    splitChunks: { cacheGroups: { default: false, common: false } },
  },
  devServer: {
    port: 4703,
    hot: true,
    static: false,
    headers: { 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*' },
    historyApiFallback: { index: '/index.html', disableDotRule: true },
  },
  plugins: [
    new NxAppRspackPlugin({
      // ... your existing options
      runtimeChunk: false,
      commonChunk: false,
    }),
    new NxReactRspackPlugin(),
    new ModuleFederationPlugin(mf),
  ],
};
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

If your bundler config is TypeScript and you now run it through the bundler CLI rather than an Nx
executor, the CLI needs its own TypeScript loader.
Install one, such as `esbuild-register`, or convert the config to CommonJS as shown above.

## Step 4: convert dynamic remotes

A workspace that loads remote URLs at runtime keeps its own name-to-URL map.
That map is not an upstream `mf-manifest.json`, so keep the file and adapt how you feed it in.
Register the remotes before the async boundary that imports your bootstrap, and translate
`loadRemoteModule(name, './Module')` into `loadRemote(name + '/Module')`.

Hosts generated on Nx 20.1 or later with `--dynamic` already call `init` or `registerRemotes` from the upstream runtime and point
their map values at each remote's `mf-manifest.json`. Leave those alone.

Every map value has to be a full entry URL. `loadRemoteModule` appended `/remoteEntry.mjs` to a
bare origin such as `http://localhost:4701`, and `registerRemotes` passes the string through
unchanged.

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Before" %}

```ts
// apps/webpack-host/src/main.ts
import { setRemoteDefinitions } from '@nx/react/mf';

fetch('/assets/module-federation.manifest.json')
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .then(setRemoteDefinitions)
  .then(() => import('./bootstrap'));
```

```tsx
// apps/webpack-host/src/app/app.tsx
import { loadRemoteModule } from '@nx/react/mf';

const RemoteA = React.lazy(() =>
  loadRemoteModule('webpackRemoteA', './Module')
);
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="After" %}

```ts
// apps/webpack-host/src/main.ts
import { registerRemotes } from '@module-federation/enhanced/runtime';

fetch('/assets/module-federation.manifest.json')
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .then((remotes: Record<string, string>) =>
    registerRemotes(
      Object.entries(remotes).map(([name, entry]) => ({
        name,
        entry,
        type: 'global' as const,
      }))
    )
  )
  .then(() => import('./bootstrap'));
```

```tsx
// apps/webpack-host/src/app/app.tsx
import { loadRemote } from '@module-federation/enhanced/runtime';

const RemoteA = React.lazy(
  () =>
    loadRemote<{ default: React.ComponentType }>(
      'webpackRemoteA/Module'
    ) as Promise<{
      default: React.ComponentType;
    }>
);
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

Both functions resolve to the module namespace, so call sites that read `m.default` or a named
export keep working. With `type: 'global'`, the runtime reads the container from
`globalThis[name]`, so each manifest key has to be the remote's normalized container name, the
same constraint the old `setRemoteDefinitions` had.

Remotes deployed under a path prefix need testing under that prefix, and no step here serves
one. `@nx/web:file-server` has no prefix option, so the prefix comes from your hosting.
A host that only ever loads `http://localhost:4701/remoteEntry.js` proves nothing about
`https://example.com/deployed/remoteEntry.js`, where both the entry and its chunks have to resolve.

A host that used `setRemoteUrlResolver` or fetched its map some other way keeps that logic and
ends it in the same `registerRemotes` call, with each resolved URL as the `entry`. Promise-based
remotes and cross-origin authentication have no tested translation here. Validate those against
your own workspace.

## Step 5: make sharing explicit

Nx derived the `shared` map from the project graph, covering framework packages, their secondary
entry points, every workspace library the app imports, and the npm dependencies of those
libraries in turn.
The official plugin shares only what you list, so write the resolved map out. The same applies
to custom configs that called `sharePackages`, `shareWorkspaceLibraries`, or `mapRemotes`
directly: those helpers have no upstream equivalent, so replace each call with the values it
produced.

```js
// apps/webpack-host/module-federation.config.js
module.exports = {
  // ... name, remotes, filename
  shared: {
    '@myorg/state': {
      singleton: true,
      requiredVersion: false,
      version: '1.0.0',
    },
    react: {
      singleton: true,
      strictVersion: true,
      requiredVersion: '^19.0.0',
      eager: true,
    },
    'react-dom': {
      singleton: true,
      strictVersion: true,
      requiredVersion: '^19.0.0',
      eager: true,
    },
    'react-dom/client': {
      singleton: true,
      strictVersion: true,
      requiredVersion: '^19.0.0',
    },
    'react/jsx-runtime': {
      singleton: true,
      strictVersion: true,
      requiredVersion: '^19.0.0',
    },
  },
};
```

Read the values from the baseline `mf-stats.json` instead of deriving them, and carry exactly
the keys it lists. For npm packages Nx emitted `singleton: true`, `strictVersion: true`, and the
`requiredVersion` range from your root `package.json`, and it marked only `react`, `react-dom`,
`react-router`, and `react-router-dom` as `eager`. Secondary entry points such as
`react/jsx-runtime` are separate keys. A key such as `pkg` does not cover `pkg/subpath` requests,
so list the subpaths the stats show. Where the baseline emitted an entry your `shared` callback or
`additionalShared` shaped, carry those values; an `additionalShared` entry the stats do not list
was never used and can go: the plugin emits a `shared` entry only for a package the app
imports. The stats list only what the app imported, so a package you start
importing later stops being shared until you add it. One field the stats rewrite: a callback
that set `requiredVersion: false` shows up as `requiredVersion: '^<version>'`, so for a workspace
library keep the `false` and `version` your callback wrote.

A source-only workspace library has no published version for the plugin to compare, and
`singleton: true` alone did not make host and remotes resolve to one copy in testing. Give it an
explicit `version`: the one the library declares, or one your team agrees on. Do not invent a
version, and do not give the same version to two different implementations. In a workspace
that links projects through package manager workspaces, Nx pinned `requiredVersion` to the
library's own `package.json` version; the stats show that, so carry it.

{% aside type="caution" title="Relative imports across library boundaries" %}
Nx installed a `NormalModuleReplacementPlugin` that rewrote any relative request reaching into
another workspace library's folder into that library's package name, which made the request
resolve to the shared module.

Once that plugin is gone, `import { state } from '../../../state/src/lib/state'` compiles and
renders exactly as before while the host and the remote each get their own copy.
A tested fixture passed its build and rendered both remotes with this bug present, and only the
shared-state assertion caught it.

Rewrite those imports to the library's public entry point, for example `@myorg/state`.
If the symbol you need is not exported from the public entry, decide explicitly whether to export
it.
{% /aside %}

## Step 6: restore the bundler settings Nx used to apply

`withModuleFederation` and `NxModuleFederationPlugin` mutated the bundler config on their way
through. Set these yourself:

| Setting                     | Value                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `output.uniqueName`         | The project name, not the normalized container name                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |
| `output.publicPath`         | `'auto'`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
| `output.scriptType`         | `'text/javascript'` on webpack                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
| `output.clean`              | `true`. The Rspack app plugin defaults it, the webpack one does not, and without it a development build lands on top of production files                                                                                                                                                   |
| `resolve.modules`           | The workspace root appended after `node_modules`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| `optimization.runtimeChunk` | `false`, and also as the app-plugin `runtimeChunk` option, which overwrites the top-level value. The wrapper let a host under the dev server keep the plugin default, and forced `'single'` on a development-mode host with several remotes; `false` there too passes the hot-update check |
| `commonChunk`               | `false` in the app-plugin options. `NxAppWebpackPlugin` defaults `commonChunk` to `true`, where `withNx()` left it unset, which adds a `common.<hash>.js` chunk to every remote                                                                                                            |
| `splitChunks.cacheGroups`   | `default` and `common` set to `false`, only if the old config used `NxModuleFederationPlugin`. `withModuleFederation` did not set them, and adding them changes the output                                                                                                                 |
| `lazyCompilation` (Rspack)  | `false`. rspack-cli dev mode defaults it on and it breaks federation                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
| `devServer.hot`             | `true` on every app. Hot updates reach the host through the remote's dev server; without it, editing a remote reloads the host and resets its state                                                                                                                                        |

The Nx dev server also derived `devMiddleware.publicPath` from `baseHref`, mapped `publicHost` to
`client.webSocketURL`, and passed `proxyConfig`, `allowedHosts`, and the `ssl` options through.
A workspace that served under a sub-path, behind a proxy, or over HTTPS needs each of those
carried into `devServer` by hand.

## Step 7: replace the serve orchestration

The official plugin federates the build and starts nothing, so the host's `serve` target no
longer brings its remotes up with it.

Point both the `build` and `serve` targets at the bundler CLI, with `NODE_ENV` standing in
for the named configurations the executor used to pass, and start the set you want explicitly.
One behavior change: the old executor set `NODE_ENV=production` for every configuration, so a
`development` build that now runs with `NODE_ENV=development` gets development-mode output such
as `react/jsx-dev-runtime`. That module is not in your stats, so each app bundles its own copy;
it imports the shared `react`, so leave it out or share it alongside `react/jsx-runtime`. Set
`NODE_ENV=production` on both configurations if you want the old output back.

```jsonc
// apps/webpack-host/project.json
{
  "targets": {
    "build": {
      "executor": "nx:run-commands",
      "outputs": ["{workspaceRoot}/dist/apps/webpack-host"],
      "defaultConfiguration": "production",
      "options": {
        "command": "webpack build --config webpack.config.js",
        "cwd": "apps/webpack-host",
      },
      "configurations": {
        "production": { "env": { "NODE_ENV": "production" } },
        "development": { "env": { "NODE_ENV": "development" } },
      },
    },
    "serve": {
      "executor": "nx:run-commands",
      "continuous": true,
      "defaultConfiguration": "development",
      "options": {
        "command": "webpack serve --config webpack.config.js",
        "cwd": "apps/webpack-host",
      },
      "configurations": {
        "development": { "env": { "NODE_ENV": "development" } },
        "production": { "env": { "NODE_ENV": "production" } },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

```shell
nx run-many -t serve -p webpack-host webpackRemoteA webpackRemoteB --parallel=3
```

Name the remotes of remotes from Step 3 in that list too; the host's `serve` starts nothing.

To work on one remote while the others stay on their last build, give every app a
`serve-static` target that serves its build output and depends on the build:

```jsonc
// apps/webpackRemoteB/project.json
{
  "targets": {
    "serve-static": {
      "executor": "@nx/web:file-server",
      "continuous": true,
      "dependsOn": ["build"],
      "options": {
        "staticFilePath": "dist/apps/webpackRemoteB",
        "port": 4702,
        "spa": true,
        "watch": false,
      },
    },
  },
}
```

If `nx.json` lists `@nx/webpack/plugin` or `@nx/rspack/plugin`, the inferred `build` and `serve`
targets work as they are, and only `serve-static` needs the explicit shape.

The official plugin writes a `.federation/` cache under each app's `node_modules`, which is
already ignored in a workspace that ignores `node_modules`.

`@nx/web:file-server` already defaults to `cors: true` and no caching. Use `staticFilePath` with
`dependsOn`, not the executor's `buildTarget` option. With `buildTarget`, the file server
spawns a nested `nx run <app>:build`, and under `nx run-many` or an e2e target that already
scheduled those builds Nx aborts with `Recursive task invocation detected`. The
`@nx/webpack/plugin` inference plugin emits the `buildTarget` form, and Nx merges `project.json`
options key by key over the inferred target, so the inferred `buildTarget` would survive next to
`staticFilePath`. If you use that plugin, set its `serveStaticTargetName` option in `nx.json` to
another name such as `serve-static-inferred`, keep this explicit target, and never run the
inferred one.

Give a remote's `serve-static` the port its production URL from Step 3 names, so a live remote
and a static one can run at the same time. Hosts, remotes without a production override, and
remotes loaded from a runtime manifest keep their existing port for both targets.

Point an e2e project's web server command at `nx run-many -t serve-static -p <host> <remotes>`,
with its URL on the host's `serve-static` port. `@nx/playwright` then warns that it could not
infer an Nx task from that command; Playwright's own `webServer` still starts the servers, so
the warning is the whole cost.

Remove `targetDefaults` entries in `nx.json` keyed on `@nx/webpack:webpack` or
`@nx/rspack:rspack`. The `NX_MF_DEV_REMOTES` input they carried is dead, and the `build`
inputs move to the generic `build` default as
`"inputs": ["production", "^production"]`.

A static host imports `<remote>/Module`, which gives the project graph a host-to-remote edge
already. A host that resolves remotes from a runtime map has no such import and no edge, so add
`implicitDependencies` on that host so the remotes build before it and `nx affected` treats a
remote change as affecting the host.
Give each app its own output directory, and make sure its build inputs cover the workspace library
sources, the federation config, and any environment variable that feeds a remote URL. `NODE_ENV`
set per configuration needs no input, since the configuration name is part of the task hash.

## Step 8: validate the result

A green build is not the acceptance criterion. The migration is done when every check you
recorded in the baseline passes again, plus these:

- Production and development configurations both build with `--skipNxCache`, production last,
  since both write the same `mf-stats.json`.
- `tsc -p apps/<app>/tsconfig.app.json --noEmit` passes for each app. The app plugin type-checks
  asynchronously, so a build exits 0 on type errors.
- Each app's production `mf-stats.json` lists the same container name, remotes, exposes, and
  `shared` entries as the baseline.
- The e2e suite passes on the replacement web server from Step 7.

Record baseline failures separately rather than weakening an assertion to get a pass.

## Step 9: remove the old package

Once no config or source file references the removed APIs, including reads of
`process.env.NX_MF_DEV_REMOTES`, which the wrapper defined and nothing defines now:

```shell
npm remove @nx/module-federation
```

`@nx/rspack` on v23 depends on `@nx/module-federation`, so on an Rspack workspace it stays in
`node_modules` and its absence from `package.json` proves nothing; `@nx/react` lists it only as an
optional peer, so on a webpack-only workspace `npm remove` takes it out. Build once with
`node_modules/@nx/module-federation` renamed out of the way if it is still there, then put it
back: `@nx/rspack` still resolves it and the next install restores it anyway. Keep `@module-federation/enhanced` in
`package.json` as a direct dependency and commit the lockfile.

## What you lose

Some of what the Nx executors did has no replacement in the official plugins today.

- **`--devRemotes` selection.** Choosing which remotes run live and which serve from a build is
  now the set of targets you start.
- **One static server for every static remote.** `@nx/react:module-federation-static-server`
  served all of them from a single port. Running a separate static server per remote is not the
  same setup, and no replacement for the consolidated server is published yet.
- **Runtime library control.** `withModuleFederation` attached a runtime plugin whenever the
  dev-server executor set `NX_MF_DEV_REMOTES`, so a live remote's copy of a shared library won
  over a static one. Nothing sets the variable or attaches the plugin now.
- **Automatic remote fallback.** Nothing here adds an error boundary. A host whose remote is
  unavailable fails the request, so add your own fallback UI if you need one.

## Tested configuration

The webpack and Rspack paths above were validated on Nx 23.2.1 with
`@module-federation/enhanced` 2.9.1, webpack 5.105.2, webpack-cli 7.2.3, Rspack 2.0.4, React
19.3.0, and Node 26.7.0 on macOS, with static and dynamic remotes, production URL overrides, a remote that consumes
another remote, deployment path prefixes, shared workspace library state, hot updates with mixed
live and static remotes, and a Playwright suite. The deployment prefix was supplied by a static
server outside Nx, since `serve-static` serves each app at its root. Expect the same file list
and federation config as the baseline, not byte-identical bundles.

## When your setup is not covered

Server-side rendering, promise-based remotes, custom runtime plugins, cross-version remote
deployments, and arbitrary `shared` callbacks were not tested. The method still applies: read
what Nx emitted from the baseline `mf-stats.json`, write it as explicit plugin options, and grade
the result in a browser against the baseline rather than on a green build. For the plugin options
themselves, use the official [configuration reference](https://module-federation.io/configure/).
